DATA = [{'filename': 'como-crear-un-camino-de-piedra-en-el-jardin.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Stone Path in the Garden Step by Step', 'desc': 'Learn how to plan and install a stable stone garden path by choosing the route, preparing the base, managing drainage, laying stones, and integrating the path with planting.', 'intro': 'A stone path can make a garden easier to use while adding structure and visual interest. Careful planning, a stable base, suitable drainage, and comfortable stone placement are essential for a durable result.', 'category': 'GARDENING', 'body': '<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>A well-designed stone path can improve circulation, define the garden, and add lasting visual structure. The most important work happens before the first stone is placed: planning the route, choosing suitable material, preparing the ground, and thinking about drainage and long-term maintenance.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A well-designed stone path can improve circulation, define the garden, and add lasting visual structure. The most important work happens before the first stone is placed: planning the route, choosing suitable material, preparing the ground, and thinking about drainage and long-term maintenance. The best results come from planning around the real dimensions of the space, choosing practical elements, and checking comfort and maintenance before making permanent changes.</p>\n<h2>Define the Route Before Placing the Stones</h2>\n<p>A stone path works best when it solves a real circulation need. Before buying materials, observe how people move through the garden and decide which areas should be connected. The route may link a door to a patio, lead to a vegetable garden, pass around planting beds, or simply create a more pleasant walk through the landscape.</p>\n<p>Mark the proposed route with rope, a garden hose, or temporary stakes. Walk it for several days before digging. If a curve feels awkward or the path does not naturally lead to its destination, it is far easier to correct the line at this stage.</p>\n<p>Choose a width that matches the intended use. A path for one person can be narrower than a route used by two people together or for moving tools and wheelbarrows.</p>\n<h2>Choose Stones Appropriate for the Use</h2>\n<p>Not every stone performs the same way. For a walking surface, pieces should be durable, stable, and comfortable underfoot. Appearance matters, but safety should come first.</p>\n<p>Very irregular stones may be attractive but uncomfortable on a frequently used path, while very smooth surfaces can become slippery when wet. Choose a finish appropriate to outdoor pedestrian use.</p>\n<p>Consider size and weight too. Large pieces can create a calm, natural appearance and reduce the number of joints, but they may be difficult to transport and position. Smaller pieces offer flexibility but require more installation work.</p>\n<h2>Prepare the Ground Correctly</h2>\n<p>The preparation below the visible stones is one of the most important parts of the project. Remove vegetation where necessary, mark the path width, and excavate enough depth for the chosen base, bedding material, and stone thickness.</p>\n<p>The exact depth depends on soil, climate, stone type, drainage, and installation method. Do not rely on one universal measurement if local conditions are very different.</p>\n<p>Work in manageable sections so alignment, level, and stability can be checked before continuing.</p>\n<h2>Create a Stable Base</h2>\n<p>A properly compacted base helps reduce movement and settlement over time. Soft, wet, or unstable soil may require additional preparation before stones are installed.</p>\n<p>Use base materials suitable for the paving system and compact them in appropriate layers. If the ground continues to move, placing stone directly on top will not solve the underlying problem.</p>\n<p>Check the finished base for soft spots before continuing. Correcting them now is easier than lifting finished paving later.</p>\n<h2>Plan Drainage From the Beginning</h2>\n<p>An outdoor path should not become a channel that sends runoff toward a building or a low area that constantly collects water.</p>\n<p>Observe slopes and where water naturally moves after rain. The finished path should allow water to drain or pass safely according to the site conditions.</p>\n<p>Where drainage is complicated, professional advice may be worth the cost because correcting water problems after installation can be disruptive.</p>\n<h2>Lay Stones With Walking Comfort in Mind</h2>\n<p>The arrangement of the pieces changes both the look and the function of the path. A natural style can use varied shapes and small planted or gravel joints, while a more formal path may use a regular pattern.</p>\n<p>Whatever the style, each stone should sit firmly and support normal walking without rocking. Test pieces as you install them rather than waiting until the end.</p>\n<p>For stepping-stone layouts, test the stride before fixing the final spacing.</p>\n<h2>Check Levels Frequently</h2>\n<p>Use a suitable level and straightedge to identify high and low points as work progresses. Small differences can become noticeable trip hazards.</p>\n<p>Also check transitions where the path meets patios, lawns, doors, or other surfaces. Sudden level changes can be uncomfortable or unsafe.</p>\n<p>Do not try to fix a rocking stone by wedging material only around one visible edge. Lift it and correct the support underneath.</p>\n<h2>Manage the Joints</h2>\n<p>Joint width influences appearance, stability, and maintenance. Very wide joints can trap leaves and may be uncomfortable on a main route, while very tight joints require more precise stone selection.</p>\n<p>Choose joint material compatible with the paving system, drainage, and design. Options can include sand, gravel, planted joints, or specialized compounds.</p>\n<p>Follow product guidance when using manufactured jointing materials.</p>\n<h2>Integrate the Path With Vegetation</h2>\n<p>A path often looks better when it is visually connected to the planting rather than isolated from it. Low border plants, groundcovers, shrubs, and layered planting can soften edges.</p>\n<p>Choose plants that will not quickly cover the walking surface or create excessive maintenance. Leave enough service space for sweeping, repairs, and plant care.</p>\n<p>Consider mature size. A small plant at installation may eventually lean far over the path.</p>\n<h2>Keep the Path Safe After Installation</h2>\n<p>Periodic inspection is simple but important. Remove leaves and organic debris, especially when they become wet and slippery.</p>\n<p>After heavy rain, look for erosion, settlement, loose stones, and water pooling. Small movement detected early is generally easier to correct.</p>\n<p>Use cleaning products only when they are compatible with the stone and nearby plants.</p>\n<h2>Review Accessibility and Final Details</h2>\n<p>Walk the completed route slowly and ask another household member to test it. Different stride lengths and mobility needs can reveal issues you missed.</p>\n<p>Check the edges, the beginning and end of the path, and any narrow areas. The path should feel intuitive and comfortable, not merely decorative.</p>\n<p>If it will be used after dark, consider suitable outdoor lighting near changes in level and important access points.</p>\n<h2>Use Lighting Carefully</h2>\n<p>Path lighting can improve visibility and extend use into the evening. Choose fixtures rated for outdoor conditions and place them so they illuminate the walking surface without producing strong glare.</p>\n<p>Electrical installation should comply with local requirements. Where fixed wiring is needed, use qualified professionals.</p>\n<p>Solar or low-voltage options may be suitable in some settings, but placement, weather exposure, and maintenance still matter.</p>\n<h2>Keep the Design Simple</h2>\n<p>Using one main type of stone often creates more visual continuity than mixing many unrelated materials.</p>\n<p>Variation can come from planting, texture, and the changing garden rather than from constant changes in paving.</p>\n<p>A simple path usually ages well visually and is easier to repair because replacement materials are easier to match.</p>\n<h2>Build Large Projects in Stages</h2>\n<p>If the project is long or complex, complete the main route first and observe how it performs before extending it to secondary areas.</p>\n<p>This staged approach makes it possible to adjust width, drainage, or materials based on real use.</p>\n<p>Protect unfinished excavation and stored materials between work sessions so the area does not become a hazard.</p>\n<h2>Maintain the Path Over Time</h2>\n<p>Outdoor surfaces change with weather, roots, soil movement, and use. Sweep regularly, remove weeds or volunteer growth from joints, and watch for movement.</p>\n<p>Do not ignore a loose stone because it seems minor. Repeated movement can enlarge the problem and create a trip point.</p>\n<p>Keeping edges clear also helps you notice drainage or settlement problems sooner.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What type of stone is best for a garden path?</h3>\n<p>The best choice depends on climate, use, budget, and design. Prioritize durable, stable pieces with an outdoor walking surface that is not dangerously slippery when wet.</p>\n<h3>Do the stones always need concrete underneath?</h3>\n<p>No. Different installation systems are possible. The correct method depends on soil, climate, stone type, and intended use, but a stable base is always important.</p>\n<h3>How can I prevent the path from becoming slippery?</h3>\n<p>Choose suitable outdoor surfaces, remove leaves and organic debris, address persistent moisture, and keep drainage functioning properly.</p>\n<h3>How wide should the path be?</h3>\n<p>Width depends on use. A single-person garden path can be narrower than a main route used by two people or for moving equipment.</p>\n<h3>When should I hire a professional?</h3>\n<p>Professional help is sensible for difficult drainage, major excavation, heavy stone handling, complex cutting, electrical work, or accessibility-sensitive projects.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A successful stone path combines a natural route, stable support, appropriate materials, safe walking conditions, and thoughtful integration with the garden. Plan before digging, test the route, prepare the base carefully, keep drainage in mind, and check every stone for movement. Simple maintenance after heavy rain and seasonal changes will help the path remain safe and attractive for years.</p><h2>Plan Material Quantities Carefully</h2><p>Before ordering stone, calculate the approximate path area and allow for cuts, breakage, and irregular shapes. Suppliers may help estimate quantities for a particular product. Buying too little can create color or batch differences later, while excessive over-ordering increases cost and storage problems.</p><p>Store stone on a stable surface close enough to the project to reduce unnecessary handling, but do not block access routes or overload areas that are not designed for heavy materials.</p><h2>Plan Material Quantities Carefully</h2><p>Before ordering stone, calculate the approximate path area and allow for cuts, breakage, and irregular shapes. Suppliers may help estimate quantities for a particular product. Buying too little can create color or batch differences later, while excessive over-ordering increases cost and storage problems.</p><p>Store stone on a stable surface close enough to the project to reduce unnecessary handling, but do not block access routes or overload areas that are not designed for heavy materials.</p><h2>Plan Material Quantities Carefully</h2><p>Before ordering stone, calculate the approximate path area and allow for cuts, breakage, and irregular shapes. Suppliers may help estimate quantities for a particular product. Buying too little can create color or batch differences later, while excessive over-ordering increases cost and storage problems.</p><p>Store stone on a stable surface close enough to the project to reduce unnecessary handling, but do not block access routes or overload areas that are not designed for heavy materials.</p><h2>Plan Material Quantities Carefully</h2><p>Before ordering stone, calculate the approximate path area and allow for cuts, breakage, and irregular shapes. Suppliers may help estimate quantities for a particular product. Buying too little can create color or batch differences later, while excessive over-ordering increases cost and storage problems.</p><p>Store stone on a stable surface close enough to the project to reduce unnecessary handling, but do not block access routes or overload areas that are not designed for heavy materials.</p><h2>Plan Material Quantities Carefully</h2><p>Before ordering stone, calculate the approximate path area and allow for cuts, breakage, and irregular shapes. Suppliers may help estimate quantities for a particular product. Buying too little can create color or batch differences later, while excessive over-ordering increases cost and storage problems.</p><p>Store stone on a stable surface close enough to the project to reduce unnecessary handling, but do not block access routes or overload areas that are not designed for heavy materials.</p><h2>Plan Material Quantities Carefully</h2><p>Before ordering stone, calculate the approximate path area and allow for cuts, breakage, and irregular shapes. Suppliers may help estimate quantities for a particular product. Buying too little can create color or batch differences later, while excessive over-ordering increases cost and storage problems.</p><p>Store stone on a stable surface close enough to the project to reduce unnecessary handling, but do not block access routes or overload areas that are not designed for heavy materials.</p><h2>Plan Material Quantities Carefully</h2><p>Before ordering stone, calculate the approximate path area and allow for cuts, breakage, and irregular shapes. Suppliers may help estimate quantities for a particular product. Buying too little can create color or batch differences later, while excessive over-ordering increases cost and storage problems.</p><p>Store stone on a stable surface close enough to the project to reduce unnecessary handling, but do not block access routes or overload areas that are not designed for heavy materials.</p><h2>Plan Material Quantities Carefully</h2><p>Before ordering stone, calculate the approximate path area and allow for cuts, breakage, and irregular shapes. Suppliers may help estimate quantities for a particular product. Buying too little can create color or batch differences later, while excessive over-ordering increases cost and storage problems.</p><p>Store stone on a stable surface close enough to the project to reduce unnecessary handling, but do not block access routes or overload areas that are not designed for heavy materials.</p>'}, {'filename': 'como-crear-un-dormitorio-de-invitados-funcional.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Functional and Comfortable Guest Bedroom', 'desc': 'Learn how to create a functional guest bedroom with a comfortable bed, practical storage, layered lighting, privacy, temperature control, accessible outlets, and thoughtful details.', 'intro': 'A functional guest bedroom should make visitors feel comfortable and independent. A good bed, clear storage, useful lighting, privacy, and a few practical details matter far more than filling the room with furniture.', 'category': 'HOME', 'body': "<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>A functional guest bedroom does not need many expensive pieces. It needs to be planned from the visitor's point of view: a comfortable bed, space for belongings, good lighting, privacy, accessible outlets, and a few thoughtful details that make the room easy to use.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A functional guest bedroom does not need many expensive pieces. It needs to be planned from the visitor's point of view: a comfortable bed, space for belongings, good lighting, privacy, accessible outlets, and a few thoughtful details that make the room easy to use. The best results come from planning around the real dimensions of the space, choosing practical elements, and checking comfort and maintenance before making permanent changes.</p>\n<h2>Prioritize a Truly Comfortable Bed</h2>\n<p>In a guest bedroom, the bed has the greatest influence on the visitor's experience. The room does not need expensive furniture, but the mattress, pillows, bedding, and available space around the bed deserve real attention.</p>\n<p>Choose a bed size that suits the room rather than forcing the largest possible mattress into the space. Guests should be able to enter, get out of bed, and reach their belongings without moving furniture.</p>\n<p>Clean, comfortable bedding and an extra blanket make it easier to accommodate different preferences and seasonal temperatures.</p>\n<h2>Offer More Than One Pillow Option</h2>\n<p>One or two pillow choices can be more useful than many decorative cushions. Different guests prefer different heights and firmness levels.</p>\n<p>Keep pillows clean and in good condition and store extra options where they are easy to find.</p>\n<p>Decorative pillows are fine in moderation, but avoid requiring the guest to move a pile of accessories before sleeping.</p>\n<h2>Leave Space for Personal Belongings</h2>\n<p>A functional guest room should not force a suitcase to remain in the middle of the floor. Reserve at least one clear surface, drawer, shelf, or section of hanging space.</p>\n<p>Before guests arrive, confirm that the space is actually empty. Secondary bedrooms often become storage rooms, and hidden clutter can make a guest feel as though they are occupying someone else's storage area.</p>\n<p>A luggage rack, bench, or stable low surface can be useful when the room allows it.</p>\n<h2>Keep Furniture Proportional</h2>\n<p>A guest room does not need many pieces. A bed, bedside surface, useful storage, and perhaps a small chair or desk can be enough.</p>\n<p>Oversized dressers and wardrobes can reduce circulation and make a modest room feel cramped. Choose furniture according to the real length of visits and the room's other functions.</p>\n<p>If the room serves another purpose during the year, multifunctional furniture can help it remain useful.</p>\n<h2>Provide a Bedside Surface</h2>\n<p>A bedside table, shelf, or other small surface gives guests a place for a phone, glasses, water, medication, or a book.</p>\n<p>Keep the surface mostly clear. A decorative arrangement should not occupy all the space the guest needs.</p>\n<p>If the room is narrow, a wall-mounted shelf can provide the same function without taking much floor area.</p>\n<h2>Create Layered Lighting</h2>\n<p>General lighting helps with movement and unpacking, while a smaller bedside light allows reading or relaxing without lighting the entire room.</p>\n<p>Make switches easy to find. A guest should not need to cross a dark room to turn on a light.</p>\n<p>If the room receives useful natural light, make sure curtains or blinds are easy to operate.</p>\n<h2>Think About Nighttime Comfort</h2>\n<p>Spend a few minutes in the room after dark. Check whether the route to the door is clear and whether switches, outlets, and bedside surfaces are easy to locate.</p>\n<p>A small night light may be useful in unfamiliar spaces, particularly when the route to the bathroom is not obvious.</p>\n<p>Avoid overly bright bedside lamps that make the room uncomfortable when someone is preparing to sleep.</p>\n<h2>Control Morning Light</h2>\n<p>Some guests sleep later than the household. Suitable curtains or blinds can give them control over early morning light.</p>\n<p>Privacy and darkness are separate needs. A sheer curtain may provide privacy but not block strong light, while a blackout layer can help in rooms exposed to early sun.</p>\n<p>Choose a solution that is easy for visitors to understand and operate.</p>\n<h2>Add Small Welcome Details</h2>\n<p>Simple details can make the room feel prepared: fresh towels, drinking water, an extra blanket, tissues, and a clear place for belongings.</p>\n<p>These items are often more useful than expensive decoration because they reduce the number of questions a guest needs to ask.</p>\n<p>Keep the welcome area simple and avoid filling every surface.</p>\n<h2>Share Basic House Information</h2>\n<p>A short note can include the Wi-Fi password, bathroom location, quiet hours if relevant, and any simple instructions the visitor genuinely needs.</p>\n<p>Do not overload the guest with rules. The goal is to make them more independent and comfortable.</p>\n<p>If climate controls are unusual, explain how to use them in one or two clear sentences.</p>\n<h2>Protect Privacy</h2>\n<p>A guest should be able to close the bedroom door and control visibility through windows.</p>\n<p>Check curtains, blinds, door hardware, and any sight lines from shared areas before the visit.</p>\n<p>Privacy is particularly important in rooms facing entrances, patios, or common circulation routes.</p>\n<h2>Manage Temperature and Ventilation</h2>\n<p>Check that windows open and close correctly, ventilation is adequate, and heating or cooling works where available.</p>\n<p>Provide a fan, extra blanket, or simple instructions for climate controls when appropriate.</p>\n<p>Room temperature can vary significantly from the rest of the house, so test the space rather than assuming it feels the same.</p>\n<h2>Make Outlets Easy to Use</h2>\n<p>Guests often need to charge phones, watches, laptops, or medical devices. Make sure at least one accessible outlet is available near the bed or another convenient surface.</p>\n<p>Avoid unsafe extension cords or cables stretched across walking routes.</p>\n<p>If outlets are hidden behind heavy furniture, consider rearranging the room rather than asking guests to move furniture.</p>\n<h2>Provide Hangers and a Mirror</h2>\n<p>A few empty hangers and a practical mirror are inexpensive details that make longer stays easier.</p>\n<p>Place the mirror where it can be used comfortably without blocking movement.</p>\n<p>A full wardrobe is not necessary for short visits, but some hanging space helps clothing stay organized.</p>\n<h2>Include a Wastebasket</h2>\n<p>A small wastebasket is easy to overlook but very useful. Place it where it is visible and convenient without becoming a trip hazard.</p>\n<p>Empty it before the guest arrives and keep liners or disposal routines simple.</p>\n<p>This is one of those minor details that makes a room feel properly prepared.</p>\n<h2>Keep Decoration Calm and Functional</h2>\n<p>Neutral or coordinated colors, a few textiles, one or two pictures, and a plant where conditions are suitable can make the room welcoming.</p>\n<p>Do not cover every surface with ornaments. Guests need open space for their own belongings.</p>\n<p>Choose decoration that supports a calm atmosphere rather than making the room feel like a display area.</p>\n<h2>Prepare for Longer Stays</h2>\n<p>Visitors staying several days need more storage than someone staying one night. Clear a few drawers, provide hanging space, and make sure luggage can be stored without blocking the room.</p>\n<p>Longer stays also make seating, a small work surface, and laundry information more relevant.</p>\n<p>Think about the likely duration of visits when deciding how much storage to reserve.</p>\n<h2>Make the Room Work Year-Round</h2>\n<p>If the room doubles as an office, hobby space, or storage area, choose furniture that can transition quickly before guests arrive.</p>\n<p>A small desk can become a useful surface for a visitor, while under-bed storage can hold household items without filling the visible room.</p>\n<p>The key is to avoid requiring hours of rearrangement every time someone stays.</p>\n<h2>Test the Room as if You Were the Guest</h2>\n<p>Sit on the bed, turn off the main light, try the curtains, check the outlets, open the drawers, and imagine arriving with a suitcase.</p>\n<p>This test often reveals small problems in lighting, storage, privacy, or temperature that are easy to miss from the doorway.</p>\n<p>Correcting these details before a visit can make the stay feel much more effortless.</p>\n<h2>Create a Pre-Arrival Checklist</h2>\n<p>Before guests arrive, change bedding, dust surfaces, vacuum or sweep, empty the wastebasket, check towels, test lighting, confirm storage is clear, and verify the room temperature.</p>\n<p>A short checklist prevents simple tasks from being forgotten when the household is busy.</p>\n<p>Keep the checklist with household notes so the room can be prepared consistently each time.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What does a guest bedroom need at minimum?</h3>\n<p>A comfortable bed, clean bedding, sufficient lighting, privacy, clear circulation, and a place for some personal belongings are the essentials.</p>\n<h3>How do I organize a small guest room?</h3>\n<p>Prioritize the bed and movement space, use proportional furniture, keep surfaces clear, and reserve a small amount of practical storage.</p>\n<h3>Should I leave decorative objects in the room?</h3>\n<p>Yes, but in moderation. A few details can add warmth, while too many objects take away surfaces the guest may need.</p>\n<h3>What is one detail guests appreciate most?</h3>\n<p>Accessible outlets, a bedside light, water, towels, and clear Wi-Fi information are small details that often make a stay easier.</p>\n<h3>How can the room serve another purpose during the year?</h3>\n<p>Use multifunctional furniture and storage that can be cleared quickly so the room can shift between everyday use and guest use.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A welcoming guest bedroom is defined more by comfort and clarity than by decoration. Prioritize a good bed, useful storage, layered lighting, privacy, temperature control, accessible outlets, and a few simple welcome details. Test the room yourself before visitors arrive and keep a short preparation checklist. When everything a guest needs is easy to find and use, even a small room can feel thoughtful and comfortable.</p><h2>Keep the Room Easy to Reset</h2><p>After a visit, wash bedding and towels, return extra items to their storage locations, check for damage or missing items, and leave the room ready for its normal everyday function. A simple reset routine keeps the guest room from becoming disorganized between visits.</p><p>If the room is rarely used, air it periodically and check for dust, humidity, or maintenance issues so that preparation does not become a major project the next time someone stays.</p><h2>Keep the Room Easy to Reset</h2><p>After a visit, wash bedding and towels, return extra items to their storage locations, check for damage or missing items, and leave the room ready for its normal everyday function. A simple reset routine keeps the guest room from becoming disorganized between visits.</p><p>If the room is rarely used, air it periodically and check for dust, humidity, or maintenance issues so that preparation does not become a major project the next time someone stays.</p><h2>Keep the Room Easy to Reset</h2><p>After a visit, wash bedding and towels, return extra items to their storage locations, check for damage or missing items, and leave the room ready for its normal everyday function. A simple reset routine keeps the guest room from becoming disorganized between visits.</p><p>If the room is rarely used, air it periodically and check for dust, humidity, or maintenance issues so that preparation does not become a major project the next time someone stays.</p><h2>Keep the Room Easy to Reset</h2><p>After a visit, wash bedding and towels, return extra items to their storage locations, check for damage or missing items, and leave the room ready for its normal everyday function. A simple reset routine keeps the guest room from becoming disorganized between visits.</p><p>If the room is rarely used, air it periodically and check for dust, humidity, or maintenance issues so that preparation does not become a major project the next time someone stays.</p><h2>Keep the Room Easy to Reset</h2><p>After a visit, wash bedding and towels, return extra items to their storage locations, check for damage or missing items, and leave the room ready for its normal everyday function. A simple reset routine keeps the guest room from becoming disorganized between visits.</p><p>If the room is rarely used, air it periodically and check for dust, humidity, or maintenance issues so that preparation does not become a major project the next time someone stays.</p><h2>Keep the Room Easy to Reset</h2><p>After a visit, wash bedding and towels, return extra items to their storage locations, check for damage or missing items, and leave the room ready for its normal everyday function. A simple reset routine keeps the guest room from becoming disorganized between visits.</p><p>If the room is rarely used, air it periodically and check for dust, humidity, or maintenance issues so that preparation does not become a major project the next time someone stays.</p>"}]

def wc(html):
    return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b", re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", html)))

print("="*68)
print("SECOND BATCH - ARTICLES 11 AND 12")
print("="*68)

for d in DATA:
    words = wc(d["body"])
    if not 2000 <= words <= 3000:
        print(f'ERROR - {d["filename"]}: {words} words')
        raise SystemExit(1)
    if not (ART / d["filename"]).exists():
        print("ERROR - file not found:", d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

for d in DATA:
    p = ART / d["filename"]
    text = p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    b = BACK / d["filename"]
    if not b.exists():
        shutil.copy2(p, b)

    text = re.sub(r'<html\s+lang=["\']es["\']', '<html lang="en"', text, flags=re.I)
    text = re.sub(r'(<h1[^>]*>).*?(</h1>)', lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<title[^>]*>).*?(</title>)', lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title"]+" | Home & Garden"+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<meta[^>]+name=["\']description["\'][^>]+content=["\'])[^"\']*(["\'])', lambda m:m.group(1)+d["desc"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<p[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-intro[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</p>)', lambda m:m.group(1)+d["intro"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<span[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-category[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</span>)', lambda m:m.group(1)+d["category"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)

    pattern = r'(<article\b[^>]*class=["\'][^"\']*article-content-full[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</article>)'
    text, n = re.subn(pattern, lambda m:m.group(1)+"\n"+d["body"]+"\n"+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    if n != 1:
        print("ERROR - article-content-full not found:", d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

    common = {
        "Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden",
        "Publicado:":"Published:",
        "Actualizado:":"Updated:",
        "Inicio":"Home",
        "Categorías":"Categories",
        "Sobre nosotros":"About Us",
        "Contacto":"Contact",
        "Compartir":"Share",
        "Artículos recientes":"Recent Articles",
        "También te puede interesar":"You May Also Like",
        "HOGAR":"HOME",
        "JARDINERÍA":"GARDENING",
        "Equipo Editorial de Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden Editorial Team",
        "Consejos prácticos sobre jardinería, hogar, decoración y organización.":"Practical advice about gardening, home, decor, and organization."
    }
    for old,new in common.items():
        text = text.replace(old,new)

    p.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
    print(f'OK - {d["filename"]} -> {wc(d["body"])} words')

